// Bridge troll, step 1 of 6: branch order is priority.
//
// Teaches: the first branch whose condition holds wins, so the branches are listed from the most important to the
// least, and an unconditional fallback goes last. A troll that sees an enemy walks over and slams it with its tree
// trunk; otherwise it patrols its bridges; with nothing to do it idles.
//
// Source: Troy Humphreys, "Exploring HTN Planners through Example", Game AI Pro (CRC Press, 2013), chapter 12,
//   section 12.3 (the "Trunk Thumper", a simplified example from Transformers: Fall of Cybertron's AI):
//   https://www.gameaipro.com/GameAIPro/GameAIPro_Chapter12_Exploring_HTN_Planners_through_Example.pdf
// Licence: the chapter is (c) CRC Press (free to read, "CRC Press retains full copyright"). Re-expressed from its
//   published description in our own words and structure; nothing is copied.
// Deviations from the source:
//   - the chapter's ChooseBridgeToCheck operator picks the bridge while the plan runs; here a host sensor publishes
//     `next_bridge` and the domain binds it, so the plan names the bridge (porting rule: target choice in the host);
//   - so the patrol branch has a condition, and an `idle` fallback is added last (the chapter's patrol is its
//     unconditional `[true]` method).
// Not ported: nothing else of section 12.3.
// Next: v2_attack_task.domain extracts the attack into a compound task with two methods.
//
// Facts (host sensors): (enemy_visible ?enemy) an enemy in sight, best target first;
//   (next_bridge ?bridge) the bridge the patrol route checks next (the host moves it on after !check_bridge).
// Primitives (logged, paper prototype): (!navigate_to ?target) (!trunk_slam ?enemy) (!check_bridge ?bridge)
//   (!idle ?seconds)
// Scenarios and expected plans: worlds/v1_priority/; stories: stories.ts. Run one with
//   npm run htn:run -- --world v1_priority/1_enemy_in_sight

(:domain BridgeTrollV1 top_level_domain

    (:method (be_troll) top_level_method

        // Most important first: an enemy in sight beats the patrol.
        (attack_enemy
            (and
                (enemy_visible ?enemy)
            )
            (
                (!navigate_to ?enemy)
                (!trunk_slam ?enemy)
            )
        )

        (patrol
            (and
                (next_bridge ?bridge)
            )
            (
                (!navigate_to ?bridge)
                (!check_bridge ?bridge)
            )
        )

        // Fallback, last and unconditional; it takes time, so an idle troll does not replan in a tight loop.
        (idle
            ()
            (
                (!idle 2.0)
            )
        )
    )
)
